# Agent completion checklist (mandatory)

Before the **final reply** on any request that used a Hub ticket or changed files, complete **all** of the following. Do not skip because the change was small, docs-only, or “quick”.

## 1. Hub time — always flush

1. Run (exact command):

```bash
node tools/hub-workflow-cli.mjs flush-ticket-time --ticket-id "<ticket-uuid>"
```

2. Or move the ticket to `qa` / `reviewer` / `done` (those stages auto-flush).
3. Confirm the CLI response includes a `time_logged` / hours value — **never** assume time was logged.
4. Do **not** invent or pass `--hours` / `time_spent`. The CLI calculates hours.
5. **Minimum billable time is 0.1 hours.** The CLI rounds any smaller amount **up to 0.1** and logs it. Never skip flush because “not enough time” or “below minimum”.
6. Report the ticket id and that time was flushed in the final response.

If you forget flush, Hub shows **0 hours** for that work — that is a process failure.

## 2. Git commit messages — describe the work

When committing (including `push:live` / “push to live” / go live):

- Subject **must** be work-based: `type(scope): short description of what changed [CHG-YYYY-DDMM-NNN]`
- Include the Change ID when one exists
- **Forbidden** subjects: `updates changelogs DDMMYYYY`, `updates`, `changes`, `wip`, `fix stuff`, or reusing an unrelated previous message
- Prefer: `npm run push:live -- "type(scope): description [CHG-ID]"`

The commit message is how humans find the work in git history. Generic changelog-date subjects are never acceptable.

## 3. Do not end the turn until

- [ ] Hub `flush-ticket-time` ran (or auto-flush via stage move), and hours were logged (≥ 0.1)
- [ ] If you committed / pushed: message describes **this** work + Change ID
- [ ] Final reply includes ticket id
